{"author":{"id":"2de4467f26c42fb223f4d3ddd5611e7c0513d3daae4c1dc315de039036bc997d"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778954128,"nonce":"s2hnEkU7ZH1Tgid+3PrjbRt26qU=","title":"Redesign margin note and sidenote architecture","message":"## Problem\nThe current note system has accumulated too many interacting layers: directive conversion, remark-side AST mutation, duplicated mobile note nodes, separate footnote reparsing, React viewport checks, float/negative-margin CSS, absolute positioning for some notes, and JS selection/highlight behavior.\n\nThis makes it hard to preserve the distinct semantics of:\n- numbered sidenotes, which have an explicit reference marker and can drift vertically to avoid collisions;\n- unnumbered margin notes, which should stay visually anchored next to the place they are authored;\n- figure captions, which should share the margin/inline behavior where appropriate;\n- notes/captions inside callouts, which should probably render inline rather than trying to escape to the desktop margin.\n\n## Context\nA review on 2026-05-16 found one concrete bug in nested `note-scope` margin notes, tracked separately in 5e0160e. That fix should unblock current content, but it does not solve the larger architectural problem.\n\nThe desired architecture should make the semantic differences explicit: sidenotes may use flow/collision-avoidance; unnumbered margin notes should be exact/author-controlled; callout-contained notes should be inline; and any JS collision avoidance should be small, isolated, and only used if needed.\n\n## Potential Solutions\n- Keep the authoring syntax, but normalize all notes/captions into a small canonical component model.\n- Parse footnotes once and share that data between desktop sidenotes and mobile/bottom footnotes.\n- Treat sidenotes and margin notes differently instead of forcing both through the same float rules.\n- Prefer CSS float/flow for numbered sidenotes; anchor unnumbered margin notes to their authored position and let authoring or explicit inline fallback handle collisions.\n- Add visual regression fixtures for dense sidenotes, dense margin notes, figures, mobile widths, and notes inside callouts.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}